{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n48 \n \n \nH. List of Preflight, In-flight, Postflight Anomalies \n3. Mission anomalies \nThere were no significant anomalies hindering Flame Design operations during \nIncrements 59-60, but there was a pertinent anomaly that had previously occurred.  \nIt was discovered on 4 Feb. 2019 (GMT 035) that the pressure was low in a check gas \nbottle for the CIR\u2019s Gas Chromatograph Instrumentation Package (GCIP).  The bottle \nhad been on the space station for 9 years, and the low pressure was attributed to a \nnominal leak rate.  The check gas was for calibration of the GCIP measurements and \nnot needed for regular operations which proceeded unhindered.  The bottle was \nreplaced on June 13 to enable the necessary calibration. \n \nOn 27 Dec. 2021 (GMT 361), during Increment 66, it was determined that the Thin \nFilament Pyrometry (TFP) fiber array was not aligned to the ACME Data Camera \n(DataCam) as intended, i.e., so that the fibers would be orthogonal relative to the \ncamera.  Unfortunately, this was the fourth day of testing in which the fiber array \nwasn\u2019t aligned, where it regrettably wasn\u2019t recognized earlier.  Given the importance \nof the TFP-derived measurements of the flame temperature, testing was halted until \nthe array was aligned (with other crew tasks) by Raja Chari on 17 Jan. 2022 (GMT 017). \n \n4. Any other difficulties in obtaining scientific data  \nFlame Design tests sometimes automatically aborted (due to the controlling software \nscript), where that was typically prior to gas flow and the activation of the igniter.  As \na result, there was little lost other than the time in the failed test attempt.   Such \naborts normally occured because of communication issues with an Image Processing \nand Storage Unit (IPSU).  These were infrequent, but there were some days when \nACME tests aborted repeatedly.  \n \nACME tests were of course also unsuccessful when the flame fails to ignite.  In the first \nround of Flame Design testing, the igniter tip failed twice requiring crew time to be \nscheduled to replace it before the subsequent test day could be conducted.  \nRegrettably, it was sometimes discovered during ACME testing that the igniter had \nfailed during the final test of the preceding test day, where an example was on 10 \nJune 2019 (19161) when no flames were ignited as a result (as discussed in section \nIII.A).  An igniter check was subsequently added to the ACME end-of-test-day", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 48}}